/-!
# Isolated subgroups and finite `p`-group separability

The theorem `isolatedFGSubgroupsPSeparable_refuted` uses Bardakov's subgroup
generated by `y` and `x y⁻¹ x⁻¹ y x` in the free group on `x` and `y`. Its elements
are represented by loops in a finite ordered graph. Reading a word preserves the order
of the vertices wherever it is defined, which makes the subgroup closed under roots.
The generators normally generate the free group, so their images generate every finite
nilpotent quotient.

## Record carried over from the retired wanted module

From `WantedExt/GroupTheory/IsolatedSubgroupPSeparableKourovka15Problem60Wanted.lean`:

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 15.60 (D. I.
Moldavanskiĭ). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701148 (`KOU-15.60`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`H` is `p'`-isolated if `xⁿ ∈ H` with `n > 0` coprime to `p` implies `x ∈ H`; `H` is separable in
finite `p`-groups if every `x ∉ H` stays outside the image of `H` under some homomorphism to a
finite `p`-group. The source asks the question for a prime `p`. The target is stated for some prime
`p` and all free groups `FreeGroup α` (the strongest true form, existential when refuted, as in
WantedExt/Sources/UnsolvedMath.md), so its refutation asserts failure for every prime: stronger than
the source's negative answer, which needs one prime, and what Bardakov proves (his isolated subgroup
is `p'`-isolated for every `p` and is not separable in the class of nilpotent groups, which contains
the finite `p`-groups).
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.IsolatedSubgroupPSeparableKourovka15Problem60Wanted

/-- **$p'$-isolated subgroup.** Let $p$ be a natural number and let $H$ be a subgroup of a group
$F$. Then $H$ is $p'$-isolated if, for every $x \in F$ and every natural number $n > 0$ that is not
divisible by $p$, $x^n \in H$ implies $x \in H$. -/
def IsPPrimeIsolated (p : ℕ) {F : Type*} [Group F] (H : Subgroup F) : Prop :=
  ∀ (x : F) (n : ℕ), 0 < n → ¬ p ∣ n → x ^ n ∈ H → x ∈ H

/-- **Separability in the class of finite $p$-groups.** Let $p$ be a natural number and let $H$ be
a subgroup of a group $F$. Then $H$ is separable in the class of finite $p$-groups if for every
$x \in F$ with $x \notin H$ there are a finite $p$-group $Q$ (a finite group in which every element
$g$ satisfies $g^{p^k} = 1$ for some natural number $k$) and a group homomorphism
$f \colon F \to Q$ with $f(x) \notin f(H)$. -/
def IsPSeparable (p : ℕ) {F : Type*} [Group F] (H : Subgroup F) : Prop :=
  ∀ x : F, x ∉ H → ∃ (Q : Type) (_ : Group Q) (_ : Finite Q), IsPGroup p Q ∧
    ∃ f : F →* Q, f x ∉ H.map f

/-- **Moldavanskiĭ's question on $p$-separability of isolated subgroups (Kourovka 15.60).** The
statement that there is a prime $p$ such that, for every set $\alpha$, every finitely generated
$p'$-isolated subgroup of the free group on $\alpha$ is separable in the class of finite
$p$-groups. -/
def isolatedFGSubgroupsPSeparable : Prop :=
  ∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧ ∀ (α : Type) (H : Subgroup (FreeGroup α)), H.FG → IsPPrimeIsolated p H →
    IsPSeparable p H

open FreeGroup

private abbrev Word := List (Bool × Bool)

-- The generators are x = of false and y = of true. Starting at vertex 2, the word
-- x y⁻¹ x⁻¹ y x runs through 0, 1, 3, 4 and back to 2. There is also a y-loop at 2.
private def bardakovWord : Word :=
  [(false, true), (true, false), (false, false), (true, true), (false, true)]

private def step : Fin 5 → Bool × Bool → Option (Fin 5)
  | 2, (false, true) => some 0
  | 3, (false, true) => some 1
  | 4, (false, true) => some 2
  | 0, (false, false) => some 2
  | 1, (false, false) => some 3
  | 2, (false, false) => some 4
  | 1, (true, true) => some 0
  | 2, (true, true) => some 2
  | 3, (true, true) => some 4
  | 0, (true, false) => some 1
  | 2, (true, false) => some 2
  | 4, (true, false) => some 3
  | _, _ => none

private def Reads (w : Word) (i j : Fin 5) : Prop := w.foldlM step i = some j

private theorem reads_append {u v : Word} {i j : Fin 5} :
    Reads (u ++ v) i j ↔ ∃ k, Reads u i k ∧ Reads v k j := by
  simp only [Reads, List.foldlM_append, Option.bind_eq_bind, Option.bind_eq_some_iff]

private theorem reads_cons {a : Bool × Bool} {w : Word} {i j : Fin 5} :
    Reads (a :: w) i j ↔ ∃ k, step i a = some k ∧ Reads w k j := by
  exact Option.bind_eq_some_iff

private theorem step_inverse : ∀ (i j : Fin 5) (a : Bool × Bool),
    step i a = some j → step j (a.1, !a.2) = some i := by decide

private theorem reads_inverse {w : Word} {i j : Fin 5} (h : Reads w i j) :
    Reads (invRev w) j i := by
  induction w generalizing i j with
  | nil =>
    obtain rfl := Option.some.inj h
    rfl
  | cons a w ih =>
    obtain ⟨k, ha, hw⟩ := reads_cons.mp h
    rw [invRev_cons, reads_append]
    exact ⟨k, ih hw, by simpa [Reads, invRev, List.foldlM] using step_inverse i k a ha⟩

private theorem reads_of_red {w v : Word} {i j : Fin 5} (hw : Reads w i j) (h : Red w v) :
    Reads v i j := by
  induction h with
  | refl => exact hw
  | tail _ hs ih =>
    cases hs with
    | not =>
      obtain ⟨k, hu, hrest⟩ := reads_append.mp ih
      obtain ⟨l, ha, hrest⟩ := reads_cons.mp hrest
      obtain ⟨m, hb, hv⟩ := reads_cons.mp hrest
      have he := step_inverse _ _ _ ha
      have : k = m := Option.some.inj (he.symm.trans hb)
      exact reads_append.mpr ⟨k, hu, this ▸ hv⟩

private def loops : Subgroup (FreeGroup Bool) where
  carrier := {g | ∃ w, Reads w 2 2 ∧ mk w = g}
  one_mem' := ⟨[], rfl, rfl⟩
  mul_mem' := by
    rintro _ _ ⟨u, hu, rfl⟩ ⟨v, hv, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨u ++ v, reads_append.mpr ⟨2, hu, hv⟩, rfl⟩
  inv_mem' := by
    rintro _ ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨invRev w, reads_inverse hw, rfl⟩

private theorem mem_loops {g : FreeGroup Bool} : g ∈ loops ↔ Reads g.toWord 2 2 := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩
    exact reads_of_red hw reduce.red
  · exact fun h => ⟨g.toWord, h, mk_toWord⟩

-- The order of the five vertices makes every letter a strictly increasing partial map.
private theorem step_compare : ∀ (a : Bool × Bool) (i j k l : Fin 5),
    step i a = some j → step k a = some l → compare i k = compare j l := by decide

private theorem reads_compare {w : Word} {i j k l : Fin 5} (h : Reads w i j)
    (h' : Reads w k l) : compare i k = compare j l := by
  induction w generalizing i k with
  | nil => rw [Option.some.inj h, Option.some.inj h']
  | cons a w ih =>
    obtain ⟨s, hs, hw⟩ := reads_cons.mp h
    obtain ⟨t, ht, hw'⟩ := reads_cons.mp h'
    exact (step_compare a i s k t hs ht).trans (ih hw hw')

-- Successive copies of a word either fix their starting point or move strictly in one direction.
private theorem reads_repetition {w : Word} (n : ℕ) {i j : Fin 5}
    (h : Reads (List.replicate (n + 1) w).flatten i j) :
    ∃ k, Reads w i k ∧ compare i k = compare i j := by
  induction n generalizing i with
  | zero => exact ⟨j, by simpa using h, rfl⟩
  | succ n ih =>
    rw [List.replicate_succ, List.flatten_cons, reads_append] at h
    obtain ⟨k, hik, hkj⟩ := h
    obtain ⟨l, hkl, hl⟩ := ih hkj
    have hdir := (reads_compare hik hkl).trans hl
    have transitive : ∀ a b c : Fin 5, compare a b = compare b c →
        compare a b = compare a c := by decide
    exact ⟨k, hik, transitive i k j hdir⟩

private theorem loops_isolated (p : ℕ) : IsPPrimeIsolated p loops := by
  -- The reduced word for a positive power is a conjugate of a repeated cyclically reduced word.
  intro g n hn _ hg
  obtain ⟨m, rfl⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_succ_of_ne_zero hn.ne'
  rw [mem_loops, toWord_pow, reduceCyclically.reduce_flatten_replicate_succ isReduced_toWord,
    reads_append] at hg
  obtain ⟨j, h, hj⟩ := hg
  obtain ⟨i, hi, hij⟩ := reads_append.mp h
  have hj' := reads_inverse hj
  rw [invRev_invRev] at hj'
  have : i = j := Option.some.inj (hi.symm.trans hj')
  subst j
  obtain ⟨k, hk, he⟩ := reads_repetition m hij
  have : i = k := compare_eq_iff_eq.mp (by simpa using he)
  subst k
  rw [mem_loops, ← reduceCyclically.conj_conjugator_reduceCyclically g.toWord]
  exact reads_append.mpr ⟨i, reads_append.mpr ⟨i, hi, hk⟩, hj⟩

private theorem y_mem_loops : of true ∈ loops := ⟨[(true, true)], rfl, rfl⟩

private theorem bardakovWord_mem_loops : mk bardakovWord ∈ loops := ⟨bardakovWord, rfl, rfl⟩

-- Labels of tree paths from the basepoint to each vertex.
private def treePath : Fin 5 → Word
  | 0 => [(false, true)]
  | 1 => [(false, true), (true, false)]
  | 2 => []
  | 3 => [(false, false), (true, false)]
  | 4 => [(false, false)]

private def generators : Set (FreeGroup Bool) := {of true, mk bardakovWord}

private theorem edge_mem_closure {i j : Fin 5} {a : Bool × Bool} (ha : step i a = some j) :
    mk (treePath i) * mk [a] * (mk (treePath j))⁻¹ ∈ Subgroup.closure generators := by
  -- Closing a tree edge gives the identity. The other two edges give the two generators.
  have labels : ∀ (s t : Fin 5) (b : Bool × Bool), step s b = some t →
      let g := mk (treePath s) * mk [b] * (mk (treePath t))⁻¹
      g = 1 ∨ g = of true ∨ g = (of true)⁻¹ ∨ g = mk bardakovWord ∨ g = (mk bardakovWord)⁻¹ := by
    decide
  have hy : of true ∈ Subgroup.closure generators := Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp [generators])
  have hw : mk bardakovWord ∈ Subgroup.closure generators :=
    Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp [generators])
  rcases labels i j a ha with h | h | h | h | h <;> rw [h]
  exacts [(Subgroup.closure generators).one_mem, hy, (Subgroup.closure generators).inv_mem hy, hw,
    (Subgroup.closure generators).inv_mem hw]

private theorem path_mem_closure {w : Word} {i j : Fin 5} (h : Reads w i j) :
    mk (treePath i) * mk w * (mk (treePath j))⁻¹ ∈ Subgroup.closure generators := by
  induction w generalizing i with
  | nil =>
    obtain rfl := Option.some.inj h
    rw [← one_eq_mk, mul_one, mul_inv_cancel]
    exact (Subgroup.closure generators).one_mem
  | cons a w ih =>
    obtain ⟨k, hak, hkj⟩ := reads_cons.mp h
    have hh := (Subgroup.closure generators).mul_mem (edge_mem_closure hak) (ih hkj)
    rw [show mk (a :: w) = mk [a] * mk w from rfl]
    simpa only [mul_assoc, inv_mul_cancel_left] using hh

private theorem loops_fg : loops.FG := by
  apply (Subgroup.fg_iff loops).mpr
  refine ⟨generators, le_antisymm ((Subgroup.closure_le _).mpr ?_) ?_, by simp [generators]⟩
  · simpa [generators, Set.insert_subset_iff] using And.intro y_mem_loops bardakovWord_mem_loops
  · rintro _ ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩
    simpa only [treePath, ← one_eq_mk, one_mul, inv_one, mul_one] using path_mem_closure hw

-- Any normal subgroup containing the loops kills y in the quotient. The other generator
-- x y⁻¹ x⁻¹ y x then reduces to x, so the quotient is trivial.
private theorem normal_eq_top (N : Subgroup (FreeGroup Bool)) [N.Normal] (h : loops ≤ N) :
    N = ⊤ := by
  let q := QuotientGroup.mk' N
  have hy : q (of true) = 1 := (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).mpr (h y_mem_loops)
  have hw : q (mk bardakovWord) = 1 := (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).mpr (h bardakovWord_mem_loops)
  change q (of false * (of true)⁻¹ * (of false)⁻¹ * of true * of false) = 1 at hw
  have hx : q (of false) = 1 := by
    simpa only [_root_.map_mul, _root_.map_inv, hy, inv_one, mul_one, mul_inv_cancel, one_mul]
      using hw
  rw [← top_le_iff, ← closure_range_of Bool, Subgroup.closure_le]
  rintro _ ⟨b, rfl⟩
  cases b
  · exact (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).mp hx
  · exact h y_mem_loops

private theorem image_eq_top {Q : Type} [Group Q] [Finite Q] [Group.IsNilpotent Q]
    (f : FreeGroup Bool →* Q) (hf : Function.Surjective f) : loops.map f = ⊤ := by
  rcases eq_top_or_exists_le_coatom (loops.map f) with h | ⟨M, hM, hle⟩
  · exact h
  · have : M.Normal :=
      Subgroup.NormalizerCondition.normal_of_coatom M Group.normalizerCondition_of_isNilpotent hM
    have h := normal_eq_top (M.comap f) (Subgroup.map_le_iff_le_comap.mp hle)
    exact (hM.1 ((Subgroup.comap_injective hf) (h.trans (Subgroup.comap_top f).symm))).elim

/--
**Isolated subgroups of free groups need not be $p$-separable.** For every prime $p$ there are a
set $\alpha$ and a finitely generated $p'$-isolated subgroup of the free group on $\alpha$ that is
not separable in the class of finite $p$-groups. That is, the statement of Kourovka Problem 15.60
fails for every prime $p$.

Bardakov proved that every nonabelian free group has a finitely generated isolated subgroup $H$
($x^n \in H$ with $n \ne 0$ implies $x \in H$) that is not separable in the class of nilpotent
groups; it is $p'$-isolated for every prime $p$, and finite $p$-groups are nilpotent. The Kourovka
Archive answers 15.60 "No, it is not".

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved
Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of
Solved Problems, Problem 15.60 (D. I. Moldavanskiĭ); resolution: V. G. Bardakov, On D. I.
Moldavanskii's question about p-separable subgroups of a free group, Siberian Math. J. 45 (2004),
no. 3, 416-419, arXiv:math/0404292, abstract (every nonabelian free group has a finitely generated
isolated subgroup that is not separable in the class of nilpotent groups); Kourovka Archive, Problem
15.60 (answer: No, it is not), https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0404292.
-/
public theorem isolatedFGSubgroupsPSeparable_refuted : ¬ isolatedFGSubgroupsPSeparable := by
  rintro ⟨p, hp, hall⟩
  have hx : of false ∉ loops := by
    rw [mem_loops, toWord_of]
    exact fun h => (by decide : (0 : Fin 5) ≠ 2) (Option.some.inj h)
  obtain ⟨Q, _, _, hQ, f, hsep⟩ := hall Bool loops loops_fg (loops_isolated p) (of false) hx
  let : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
  let := (hQ.to_subgroup f.range).isNilpotent
  have hmem : f.rangeRestrict (of false) ∈ loops.map f.rangeRestrict := by
    rw [image_eq_top f.rangeRestrict f.rangeRestrict_surjective]
    exact Subgroup.mem_top _
  obtain ⟨g, hg, he⟩ := hmem
  exact hsep ⟨g, hg, congrArg Subtype.val he⟩

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.IsolatedSubgroupPSeparableKourovka15Problem60Wanted
